/* data/learn-c/phy-12-03.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-03.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-03"] = {"3.1":{"s":"Boyle's, Charles' and Gay-Lussac's laws combine into one equation of state for a fixed amount of gas: PV = nRT. The same law can be written with the number of molecules N and Boltzmann's constant. This chapter explains these gas laws from the motion of molecules (kinetic theory), then moves on to heat radiation from hot bodies.","f":[{"x":"P*V = n*R*T","n":"Ideal gas equation; n = moles, R = 8.314 J/(mol K).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.1, p.56"},{"x":"P*V = N*k_B*T","n":"Same law with N = number of molecules; k_B = Boltzmann constant.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.1, p.56"},{"x":"R = N_A*k_B","n":"N_A = Avogadro number.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.1, p.56"}],"w":["Temperature must be in kelvin in every gas law. Using Celsius gives wrong answers.","Use the same units for pressure and volume on both sides of a ratio."],"x":["In Class 11 you met three gas laws for a fixed mass of gas. Boyle's law: at constant temperature, PV is constant. Charles's law: at constant pressure, V is proportional to the absolute temperature T. Gay-Lussac's law: at constant volume, P is proportional to T.","The three combine into one equation of state, PV = nRT, for n moles of gas. R = 8.314 J/(mol K) is the universal gas constant. Another form is PV = NkT, with N the number of molecules and k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J/K the Boltzmann constant.","This chapter explains these laws by looking at the molecules. Kinetic theory links the pressure and temperature that we feel to the random motion of billions of molecules. The second part of the chapter is about heat radiation."],"e":"One mole of gas at 273 K and 1 atm (1.013 × 10⁵ Pa) has V = nRT/P = 8.314 × 273/1.013 × 10⁵ = 0.0224 m³, or 22.4 litres."},"3.2":{"s":"A gas has a huge number of molecules (about 10²⁵ per cubic metre) moving at random, so following each one with Newton's laws is hopeless. Kinetic theory makes simple assumptions about the molecules and works with averages, then links those averages to the large-scale quantities P, V, T and energy.","f":[{"x":"P = ⅔ × (KE per unit volume)","n":"Pressure and kinetic energy per volume. Ideal gas, from kinetic theory."}],"w":["Kinetic theory gives averages. It does not say each molecule has the same speed or energy.","Pressure comes from molecules hitting the walls, not from them pushing each other."],"x":["A gas contains a huge number of molecules, about 10²⁵ in each cubic metre at room conditions. They move at random in all directions and collide with each other and with the walls. It is impossible to follow each molecule, so kinetic theory uses averages.","The model makes simple assumptions. The molecules are very small compared with the distances between them, so the gas is mostly empty space. They exert no forces on each other except during collisions. All collisions are elastic, so kinetic energy is conserved. Between collisions they move in straight lines at constant speed, and gravity is ignored.","From these assumptions we derive the pressure, the meaning of temperature and the gas laws. The model works best for gases at low pressure and high temperature."],"e":"At room temperature the molecules of air move at several hundred metres per second, yet a smell from the kitchen takes seconds to reach you. They collide so often (billions of times per second) that each one moves in a zigzag."},"3.3":{"s":"An ideal gas has no forces between its molecules and obeys PV = nRT at all pressures and temperatures. Real gases have molecular attractions and molecules of finite size. They behave nearly ideally at low pressure and high temperature, when the molecules are far apart, as long as the gas is well above its liquefaction temperature.","f":[{"x":"PV = nRT = NkT","n":"Ideal gas equation"},{"x":"P = P₁ + P₂ + …","n":"Dalton's law of partial pressures. Non-reacting ideal gases in a mixture."}],"w":["No gas is perfectly ideal. The ideal gas law is a good guide at low pressure and high temperature.","Real gases deviate most near their liquefaction point."],"x":["An ideal gas is a model gas that obeys PV = nRT at all temperatures and pressures. Its molecules are points, with no forces between them. The internal energy of an ideal gas is just the kinetic energy of its molecules, so it depends only on the temperature.","No real gas is ideal. Real molecules have a size and they attract each other. At high pressure the molecules are close together, so the size and the attractions matter. At low temperature the attractions matter because the slow molecules can stick together, and the gas finally liquefies.","A real gas behaves almost like an ideal one at low pressure and high temperature, where the molecules are far apart and fast. Air, hydrogen and helium at room conditions are very close to ideal. Gases near their liquefaction point, like steam close to 100 °C, are not."],"e":"Nitrogen at 1 atm and 300 K follows PV = nRT to better than 0.1%. Steam at 1 atm and 105 °C does not follow it as well."},"3.4":{"s":"Gas molecules move in straight lines between collisions. The mean free path is the average distance a molecule travels between two collisions. It is shorter when the gas is denser and when the molecules are bigger: it varies as 1/d², not 1/d. Lowering the pressure, as in vacuum coating of metals, makes it longer.","f":[{"x":"lambda = 1/(sqrt(2)*pi*d^2*(N/V))","n":"Mean free path; d = molecular diameter, N/V = number of molecules per unit volume.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.4, p.57"}],"w":["Doubling the molecule's diameter cuts the mean free path to a quarter."],"x":["A gas molecule moves in a straight line until it hits another molecule, then it changes direction. The mean free path λ is the average distance it travels between two successive collisions.","If the molecules are spheres of diameter d and there are n molecules per unit volume, then λ = 1/(√2 π d² n). A molecule meets others more often if they are large (large d) or crowded (large n), so λ is shorter. In terms of pressure and temperature, n = P/kT, so λ = kT/(√2 π d² P). A higher pressure gives a shorter mean free path.","In air at normal pressure λ is about 10⁻⁷ m, many times the molecular size. In a good vacuum it can be as long as the vessel, so the molecules fly from wall to wall without meeting each other. That is why in a vacuum tube a beam of electrons can move without scattering."],"e":"If the pressure of a gas is halved at constant temperature, n is halved and the mean free path doubles."},"3.5":{"s":"Molecules hit the walls of the container and bounce back elastically, each hit giving the wall a small push. Adding up the momentum change per second over all molecules gives the pressure. It depends on the number of molecules per unit volume, their mass and the average of the square of their speed. The result holds for any shape of container.","f":[{"x":"P = (1/3)*(N/V)*m*<v^2>","n":"Pressure of an ideal gas; <v^2> = mean of the squared speeds, m = mass of one molecule.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.5, p.59"}],"w":["The average used is of v², not of v: the mean square speed."],"x":["Pressure comes from molecules hitting the wall. A molecule of mass m moving at speed v_x perpendicular to a wall bounces back elastically and gives the wall a momentum 2mv_x. A huge number of molecules hit every second, so the average force is steady.","Count the hits on an area A in a short time and average over all directions (a third of the motion is along each axis). The result is P = ⅓ ρ ⟨v²⟩ = ⅓ (N/V) m⟨v²⟩, where ρ is the density and ⟨v²⟩ is the mean of the squared speeds. Equivalently, PV = (2/3) × (total translational kinetic energy).","So the pressure is higher when there are more molecules per unit volume, when they are heavier, or when they move faster. Doubling the number density doubles the pressure (Boyle's law at fixed energy). Pressure has no connection with the weight of the gas; it exists in a gas in a spacecraft too."],"e":"A gas of density 1.2 kg/m³ with an rms speed of 500 m/s has P = ⅓ × 1.2 × 500² = 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa."},"3.6":{"s":"The root mean square (rms) speed is the square root of the mean of the squared molecular speeds. From the pressure formula and PV = nRT it depends only on temperature and molar mass. Lighter molecules move faster at the same temperature: at 300 K nitrogen molecules average about 517 m/s and oxygen about 483 m/s. Molecular speeds are spread out (Maxwell's distribution).","f":[{"x":"v_rms = sqrt(3*R*T/M_0)","n":"rms speed; M_0 = molar mass in kg/mol, T in kelvin.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.6, p.60"}],"w":["Use the molar mass in kg/mol: 28 g/mol for N₂ is 0.028 kg/mol.","The rms speed ∝ √T: doubling the speed needs four times the absolute temperature."],"x":["Molecules in a gas have many different speeds. To describe the typical speed, we use the root mean square (rms) speed: square every speed, take the average, then take the square root. In symbols, v_rms = √⟨v²⟩.","From P = ⅓ρv² and the gas law, v_rms = √(3P/ρ) = √(3kT/m) = √(3RT/M), where m is the mass of one molecule and M the molar mass. It grows with the square root of the absolute temperature and falls with the square root of the molar mass.","Hydrogen (M = 2 g/mol) therefore has molecules four times as fast as oxygen (M = 32 g/mol) at the same temperature. That is why light gases escape from the atmosphere of a planet more easily. The rms speed is a little larger than the average speed, which is larger than the most probable speed."],"e":"Oxygen at 300 K: v_rms = √(3 × 8.314 × 300/0.032) = 484 m/s. The same gas at 1200 K has twice that speed, about 968 m/s."},"3.7":{"s":"Combining PV = (2/3)E with the gas law shows that the average kinetic energy of a gas molecule is proportional to the absolute temperature. So temperature is a measure of the average random kinetic energy of the molecules; for an ideal gas, all of the internal energy is this kinetic energy.","f":[{"x":"E/N = (3/2)*k_B*T","n":"Average translational kinetic energy per molecule.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.7, p.60"},{"x":"P*V = (2/3)*E","n":"E = total translational kinetic energy of the gas.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.7, p.60"}],"w":["The average KE per molecule at a given T is the same for every gas; only the speed differs with mass."],"x":["Compare PV = (2/3)E, where E is the total translational kinetic energy of the N molecules, with the gas law PV = NkT. Then E = (3/2)NkT, so the average translational kinetic energy of one molecule is (1/2)m⟨v²⟩ = (3/2)kT.","This gives temperature a clear meaning in kinetic theory. How hot a gas is, on the Kelvin scale, tells you how much translational energy its molecules carry on average. Double the Kelvin temperature and the average energy doubles too.","Notice that the energy depends only on T, not on the kind of gas. At the same temperature, helium and oxygen molecules have the same average kinetic energy, but the lighter helium molecules move faster. At absolute zero, the kinetic energy of the ideal gas would be zero."],"e":"At 300 K, the average kinetic energy of a molecule is 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300 = 6.2 × 10⁻²¹ J, about 0.039 eV."},"3.8":{"s":"Each independent way a molecule can store energy (a degree of freedom, giving one squared term in its energy) gets on average ½ kT. A monatomic molecule has 3 translational degrees of freedom. A rigid diatomic molecule adds 2 rotational ones (rotation about its own axis does not count). A vibrating molecule adds a kinetic and a potential term for each vibration mode. The law fails at very low temperatures, where quantum effects matter.","f":[{"x":"E = (1/2)*k_B*T","n":"Average energy for each quadratic term (degree of freedom) in thermal equilibrium.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.8, p.62"}],"w":["Each vibration mode counts twice (kinetic + potential), so it adds kT, not ½kT."],"x":["A degree of freedom is an independent way in which a molecule can hold energy. The law of equipartition says: in thermal equilibrium at temperature T, every degree of freedom (every independent squared term in the energy) has an average energy of ½kT per molecule.","A monatomic molecule (He, Ne, Ar) can only move in three directions, so f = 3 and the average energy is (3/2)kT. A diatomic molecule (H₂, N₂, O₂), treated as rigid, can also rotate about two axes, so f = 5 and the average energy is (5/2)kT. At high temperatures the molecule can also vibrate, which adds more degrees of freedom.","For one mole, the internal energy of an ideal gas is U = (f/2)RT. From this, C_v = (f/2)R and C_p = C_v + R = ((f + 2)/2)R, so γ = C_p/C_v = 1 + 2/f. This gives γ = 5/3 for monatomic and 7/5 for diatomic gases, in good agreement with experiments at room temperature."],"e":"One mole of nitrogen (f = 5) at 300 K has U = (5/2) × 8.314 × 300 = 6236 J."},"3.9":{"s":"A gas has two specific heats: at constant volume and at constant pressure. At constant pressure the gas also does work as it expands, so more heat is needed: Mayer's relation says the molar values differ by R. Using equipartition, the molar specific heats, and their ratio γ, follow from the number of degrees of freedom: monatomic, rigid diatomic, vibrating diatomic and polyatomic gases all give different values.","f":[{"x":"C_p - C_v = R","n":"Mayer's relation for molar specific heats.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.9, p.63"},{"x":"S_p - S_v = R/(M_0*J)","n":"Principal (per kg) specific heats with heat in calories; J = mechanical equivalent of heat.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.9, p.63"},{"x":"C_v = (3/2)*R","n":"Monatomic gas; C_p = (5/2)R and gamma = 5/3.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.9, p.63"},{"x":"C_v = (5/2)*R","n":"Rigid diatomic gas; C_p = (7/2)R and gamma = 7/5.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.9, p.64"},{"x":"gamma = (4 + f)/(3 + f)","n":"Polyatomic gas with f vibrational modes.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.9, p.64"}],"w":["Cp is always larger than Cv for a gas, so γ is always more than 1."],"x":["A gas has two main molar specific heats. C_v is the heat needed to raise the temperature of one mole by 1 K at constant volume. C_p is the same at constant pressure.","At constant volume the gas does no work, so all the heat goes into internal energy: Q = C_v ΔT. At constant pressure the gas expands and does work PΔV = RΔT on its surroundings, so more heat is needed: C_p = C_v + R. This is Mayer's relation. It holds for every ideal gas, so C_p is always larger than C_v.","The ratio γ = C_p/C_v depends on the type of molecule: 5/3 (about 1.67) for monatomic gases and 7/5 (1.4) for rigid diatomic gases. The value of γ also appears in the speed of sound and in adiabatic processes (PV^γ = constant), which you meet in thermodynamics."],"e":"For a diatomic gas, C_v = (5/2)R = 20.8 J/(mol K) and C_p = (7/2)R = 29.1 J/(mol K), so γ = 1.4."},"3.10":{"s":"When thermal radiation falls on an object, part of it is absorbed, part is reflected and part passes through. The fractions of the incident energy are the coefficients of absorption a, reflection r and transmission t, and they add up to 1. Glass and quartz let heat radiation through (diathermanous); wood and iron do not (athermanous). The coefficients also depend on the wavelength of the radiation.","f":[{"x":"a + r + t_r = 1","n":"The three fractions of the incident heat energy Q: a = Q_a/Q (absorbed), r = Q_r/Q (reflected), t_r = Q_t/Q (transmitted). They have no unit.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.10, p.65"}],"w":["For an opaque object t = 0, so a + r = 1: a good absorber is a poor reflector.","A perfect blackbody has a = 1, so r = 0 and t = 0."],"x":["Heat can travel by conduction, convection and radiation. Radiation is the transfer of heat by electromagnetic waves, mostly infrared. It needs no medium: the heat of the Sun reaches the Earth across empty space.","When thermal radiation falls on a surface, three things can happen. A fraction is absorbed (the absorptivity a), a fraction is reflected (the reflectivity r) and a fraction passes through (the transmissivity t). They add up to one: a + r + t = 1.","A dull black surface absorbs most radiation and reflects little. A shiny polished surface reflects most of it. A clear glass transmits visible light and a large part of the near-infrared radiation of the Sun, but blocks the longer-wavelength infrared radiation from objects at room temperature. This is the basis of the greenhouse effect."],"e":"A white shirt reflects much of the sunlight and feels cooler than a black one, which absorbs most of it and warms up."},"3.11":{"s":"A perfect blackbody absorbs all radiation falling on it (absorptivity 1) and reflects none. Lamp black comes close. Good absorbers are also good emitters, and good reflectors are poor emitters, which is why vacuum flasks are silvered. Ferry's blackbody is a double-walled hollow sphere, blackened inside, with a small hole: radiation that enters is reflected many times and absorbed, so the hole acts as a blackbody. A heated cavity gives out blackbody (cavity) radiation.","w":["It is the small hole, not the sphere, that behaves as the blackbody."],"x":["A perfect blackbody is an ideal body that absorbs all the radiation that falls on it, at every wavelength: a = 1, r = 0, t = 0. It would look black at ordinary temperatures if nothing is shining on it.","A good practical blackbody is a hollow object with a tiny hole (Ferry's blackbody). Radiation that enters through the hole bounces around inside, and each time a little is absorbed, so almost none comes out again. Lamp black (soot) and a black-painted cavity come close.","A blackbody is also the best possible emitter at every temperature. The radiation it sends out is called blackbody radiation, and its spectrum depends only on its temperature, not on the material. The Sun and the stars are nearly blackbodies, and so is the glowing inside of a furnace."],"e":"Through a small window, the inside of a hot furnace looks bright and without details: all the walls glow equally, as a blackbody at that temperature does."},"3.12":{"s":"Prevost's theory: every body above 0 K radiates and absorbs heat all the time. A body stays at constant temperature when it absorbs as much as it emits. The emissive power is the energy radiated per unit time per unit area. Emissivity compares a surface with a perfect blackbody at the same temperature: 1 for a blackbody, 0 for a perfect reflector, in between for real surfaces.","f":[{"x":"R = Q/(A*t)","n":"Emissive power: energy Q radiated per unit area per unit time (W/m^2).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.12, p.67"},{"x":"e = R/R_B","n":"Emissivity: emissive power compared with a perfect blackbody's at the same temperature.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.12, p.68"}],"w":["A body at room temperature still radiates; it also absorbs the same amount from its surroundings."],"x":["Prevost's theory of exchange says that every body above absolute zero emits heat radiation all the time, and absorbs the radiation that falls on it. A body that is hotter than its surroundings emits more than it absorbs, and cools. A body that is colder absorbs more than it emits, and warms.","When the temperature of a body equals that of its surroundings, it still emits and absorbs, but at the same rate. This is a dynamic thermal equilibrium: there is no net exchange of heat.","The emissive power of a surface is the energy it radiates per unit area per second. At a given temperature it is largest for a blackbody. The emissivity (e) of a surface is the ratio of its emissive power to that of a blackbody at the same temperature, so 0 ≤ e ≤ 1. A dull black surface has e close to 1, and a polished metal surface has a small e."],"e":"A cup of hot tea left in a cold room loses heat to the room by radiation, while also absorbing a smaller amount from the walls. The net loss falls as the tea cools."},"3.13":{"s":"Kirchhoff's law: at a given temperature, the ratio of a body's emissive power to its absorptivity is the same for all bodies and equals the emissive power of a perfect blackbody. So a body's emissivity equals its absorptivity, wavelength by wavelength. A good absorber of some radiation is a good emitter of the same radiation.","f":[{"x":"a = e","n":"Absorptivity equals emissivity at the same temperature (and wavelength).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.13, p.68"}],"w":["This is why cooking pots are blackened at the bottom (absorb well) and polished on top."],"x":["Kirchhoff's law of heat radiation says that, at a given temperature, the ratio of the emissive power of a body to its absorptivity is the same for all bodies, and equals the emissive power of a perfect blackbody at that temperature. In short: good absorbers are good emitters, and poor absorbers are poor emitters.","Put e for the emissive power and a for the absorptivity. Then e/a = E_blackbody. For a blackbody a = 1, so e = E_blackbody, the largest value. For any other body a is less than 1, so e is less than E_blackbody.","Everyday examples: a polished silver teapot keeps tea hot longer than a dull black one, since it emits less. A black surface takes up solar energy and also loses it quickly at night. A piece of china with a dark pattern, heated red-hot and viewed in a dark room, shows the pattern brighter than the white parts, because the dark parts are better emitters."],"e":"A body absorbs 40% of the radiation that falls on it (a = 0.4). By Kirchhoff's law its emissive power is 0.4 times that of a blackbody at the same temperature."},"3.14":{"s":"A blackbody's radiation is spread over many wavelengths. At each temperature the power rises with wavelength to a peak and then falls. As temperature rises, the total power (area under the curve) increases and the peak moves to shorter wavelengths: Wien's displacement law. Classical theories failed to explain the whole curve; Planck succeeded by assuming energy comes in quanta hν. Wien's law gives the temperature of the Sun and stars from their colour.","f":[{"x":"lambda_max*T = b","n":"Wien's displacement law; b = 2.897 × 10^-3 m K, T in kelvin.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.14, p.70"}],"w":["λmax is the wavelength of greatest intensity, not the longest wavelength emitted."],"x":["A blackbody does not radiate at only one wavelength. It sends out a continuous spread. At a fixed temperature the power per wavelength rises from zero at very short wavelengths, reaches a peak at a wavelength λ_m, and falls again at long wavelengths.","Wien's displacement law says that the peak wavelength is inversely proportional to the absolute temperature: λ_m T = b, with b = 2.898 × 10⁻³ m K. As a body gets hotter, the peak moves to shorter wavelengths, and the colour changes from dull red to orange to yellow and then white. The Sun, at about 5800 K, peaks near 500 nm (green-yellow), while a person at 310 K peaks near 9400 nm, in the infrared.","The area under the whole curve is the total power radiated. When the temperature rises, the whole curve rises, and the area increases very fast, as shown in the next topic (Stefan–Boltzmann law). Classical physics could not explain the shape of these curves at short wavelengths, and Planck solved the problem by introducing energy quanta, which started quantum theory."],"e":"For a hot object at 3000 K, λ_m = 2.898 × 10⁻³/3000 = 966 nm, in the near infrared. It looks orange-red, because only the tail of its spectrum is visible."},"3.15":{"s":"A hot body gives out heat radiation at a rate that grows very fast with its temperature. Stefan-Boltzmann law: the power radiated per unit area by a perfect blackbody is proportional to the fourth power of its absolute temperature. An ordinary body radiates a fraction e (its emissivity) of that. In surroundings at temperature T₀, the net loss depends on the difference of the fourth powers.","f":[{"x":"R = sigma*T^4","n":"Radiant power per unit area of a perfect blackbody; sigma = Stefan's constant = 5.67 × 10^-8 W m^-2 K^-4; T in kelvin.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.15, p.71"},{"x":"Q/(A*t) = sigma*T^4","n":"The same law as energy Q radiated in time t from area A.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.15, p.71"},{"x":"R = e*sigma*T^4","n":"An ordinary (not black) body; e = emissivity, between 0 and 1.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.15, p.71"},{"x":"R_net = e*sigma*(T^4 - T_0^4)","n":"Net power per unit area lost by a body at T in surroundings at T_0.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.15, p.71"}],"w":["Use kelvin, and take the fourth powers first: T⁴ − T₀⁴, not (T − T₀)⁴.","Doubling the absolute temperature makes the radiated power 16 times larger."],"x":["The total energy radiated per second per unit area by a perfect blackbody is proportional to the fourth power of its absolute temperature: E = σT⁴. This is the Stefan–Boltzmann law, and σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W/(m² K⁴) is the Stefan–Boltzmann constant.","For a body of surface area A at temperature T, with emissivity e, the power emitted is P = eσAT⁴. A body at temperature T in surroundings at temperature T₀ loses net power eσA(T⁴ − T₀⁴).","The fourth power makes a big difference. If you double the absolute temperature, the radiated power becomes 16 times larger. That is why a red-hot object loses heat so much more quickly than a warm one, and why the Sun, at about 5800 K, radiates so much energy.","Use kelvin in this formula, never degrees Celsius. This is one of the most common mistakes."],"e":"A blackbody of area 0.5 m² at 1000 K radiates P = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ × 0.5 × 10¹² = 28 350 W."}};
